!!id:"https://tson.io/test-suite/schemas/vocabulary-sidecar.tn"
!!meta:"https://tson.io/2026/35/m/meta.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/35/m/core.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/test-suite/schemas/sidecar-common.tn"
@doc:"""
  Formal shape of a vocabulary-layer sidecar (tests/class1/vocabulary/**/*-expected.tn) -- the
  built-in type vocabulary of §5. See README.md's "Vocabulary-layer vectors" and RUNNER.md.

  `type-ref` sits outside the outcome group because both outcomes need it: a runner cannot apply an
  atom's contract without knowing which atom.
  """
{
  complex_value => {
    real: text
    imaginary: text
  }

  @doc:"""
    The expected host value, host-representation-neutral: §5.2 leaves the concrete bound type
    implementation-defined, so a vector states the value in whichever textual form every
    implementation can compare against, and names which form it used.

    - `decimal`  a plain decimal string -- the whole integer ladder, number, float32/float64
    - `hex`      a hex string of the raw bytes -- base64/base64url/base32/hex, and ipv6's address
    - `rational` "numerator/denominator"
    - `text`     the value's canonical text form -- text, uri, uuid, the temporal family, ipv4,
                 cidr4/cidr6, mac, email
    - `complex`  real and imaginary parts, each a decimal string

    `duration` and `period` use `decimal`, not `text`: their values are a signed number of
    seconds and a signed number of months respectively, so `PT1H30M` is `5400` and `P1Y2M` is
    `14`. Stating the value rather than a canonical spelling is what keeps them comparable --
    `P3W`, `PT504H` and `P21D` are one duration, and no implementation's choice of output form
    is privileged.

    The flat `value: text?` this replaces could not express complex at all, though real
    sidecars have always written it as a nested record.
    """
  atom_value => {
    ( decimal: text
    | hex: text
    | rational: text
    | text: text
    | complex: complex_value )
  }

  vocabulary_valid => {
    value: atom_value
  }

  @doc:"""
    An `error` vector's category is `resolver` where the atom's grammar rejected the token and
    `validation` where a correctly-shaped value fell outside a declared bound -- §8.1 assigns range
    violations by the numeric atoms to validation explicitly. Which category governs a *format*
    rejection is not yet settled by the spec; see README.md.
    """
  vocabulary_sidecar => sidecar_common & {
    type-ref: text
    ( valid: vocabulary_valid | error: vector_error )
  }
}
